<comment datatype='string'>&lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=ENZYME&amp;object=CPLX-7788&quot;&gt;Acetyl-CoA &lt;i&gt;C&lt;/i&gt;-acetyltransferase&lt;/a&gt; was purified and characterized from the extremely halophilic archaeon &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-157834&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Halobacterium&lt;/i&gt; sp. &lt;i&gt;JP-6&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/12013440&quot;&gt;Liu02a&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
The native enzyme had a molecular mass of 80 kDa, and consisted of two identical subunits of 43kDa each. The purified enzyme had a very narrow optimal pH range around of 7.9 for acetoacetyl-CoA thiolysis. The thiolytic activity was inhibited by the presence of &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=MG%2b2&quot;&gt;Mg&lt;SUP&gt;2+&lt;/SUP&gt;&lt;/a&gt; -at 20mM only 40% of the activity remained. On the other hand, the enzyme was stimulated by KCl or NaCl.
&lt;p&gt;
The distinct kinetic profile of the enzyme indicates that it may have a different catalytic mechanism from the so-called ping-pong mechanism employed by other thiolases [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/12013440&quot;&gt;Liu02a&lt;/a&gt;].</comment>
